<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Francia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> was the western kingdom created by the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 CE, assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. It roughly corresponded to the western and central parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Accounts of the Carolingian Empire treat it as one of the successor kingdoms that emerged from the empire's fragmentation, and it later developed into the Kingdom of France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-francia.jpg" alt="West Francia" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: maix¿?; derivative work by Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Francia_843.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>, or the Kingdom of the West Franks, was created from the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. In August 843, after three years of civil war, his three sons signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a>. The youngest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, received the western part, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> and Louis the German East Francia. The West Frankish Annales Bertiniani say the rest, as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was ceded to Charles. It did not include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, Provence or Brittany. Aquitaine was assigned to Charles by the treaty, but he had to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin II</a> of Aquitaine, and by 849 had secured most of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>West Frankish kings were elected by the secular and ecclesiastical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> was crowned Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 875. After the death of Carloman II in 884 the nobles elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, already king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and for a time all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> was reunited under one ruler. His handling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 885–86 reduced his prestige, and he was deposed in 887. The nobles then elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-of-france">Odo, Count of Paris</a>, as king. Between 888 and 936 candidates from the Carolingian and Robertian houses were chosen alternately, and the kings grew weaker as regional dukes and nobles became semi-independent. Duchies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Aquitaine, Normandy and Champagne emerged.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> settled in Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> invaded from 919. The nobles deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> in 922, and he died in captivity in 929. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>, who inherited his father Louis IV's lands in 954 under his mother Gerberga's regency, the royal lands were so small that the Carolingian practice of dividing them among the sons was dropped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> and his son Louis V died prematurely, the nobles elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> king in 987. This traditionally marks the end of the Carolingian line in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the end of West Francia as a kingdom. Hugh was the first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">House of Capet</a>, which ruled France through the High Middle Ages, though at first they controlled little beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>. Historians generally see the period as the gradual transition toward the Kingdom of France. By the 13th century the term Regnum francorum had evolved into Regnum Francia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Francia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into <b>West Francia</b> under Charles the Bald, Middle Francia (from the Low Countries through Burgundy to Italy) under Lothair, and East Francia under Louis the German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As royal authority in <b>West Francia</b> weakened, the counts gathered rights over Quercy, Albi, Nîmes and Narbonne, and in the tenth century Raymond III Pons appears in some documents with the title of duke of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Burgundy took shape in the late ninth and early tenth centuries in the western part of the old Burgundian lands, the region of Autun, Dijon, Beaune and Chalon-sur-Saône that remained within <b>West Francia</b> when Provence and Upper Burgundy formed separate kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Rollo’s failed attack on Chartres in 911, King Charles the Simple of <b>West Francia</b> granted him lands around Rouen in return for defending the Seine against other raiders and accepting baptism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of France grew out of <b>West Francia</b>, the western share of the Carolingian Empire created by the Treaty of Verdun in 843.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun in August 843 divided the realm into three kingdoms: <b>West Francia</b> under Charles, East Francia under Louis, and Middle Francia, running from the North Sea to Italy, under Lothar, who kept the imperial title.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
